\begin_layout Standard
Vectors,
 in quantum mechanics,
 are going to represent physical states of affairs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Vector space
\series default
:
 Think of a coordinate system with a specified origin point.
 Every distinct geometrical point in the space mapped out by such a coordinate system can be associated with some particular (and distinct) vector.
 The infinite collection of vectors associated with all the points in such a space is referred to as a vector space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 orthogonal simply means perpendicular (
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A|B\right\rangle =0$
\end_inset

).
 The dimension of a space is equal to the number of mutually perpendicular directions in which vectors can point within that space.
 If we have a set of vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|A_{1}\right\rangle ,\dots,\left|A_{N}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 that form a basis for an N-dimensional space,
 then any vector in this space can be expressed as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|B\right\rangle =\sum^{N}_{i=1}b_{i}\left|A_{i}\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}=\left\langle A_{i}|B\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 In general,
 for two vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|C\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 we can write:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\langle B|C\right\rangle  & =\sum^{N}_{i=1}b^{*}_{i}c_{i} & \left|B\right\rangle +\left|c\right\rangle  & =\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(b_{i}+c_{i}\right)\left|A_{i}\right\rangle 
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can also represent a vector as a column vector by writing 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|B\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
b_{1} & \dots & b_{N}\end{array}\right]^{T}$
\end_inset

.
